{
  "analysis": "I'm in the /workspace directory and need to create a TED-style talk outline. I can see there are several files including conversation_notes.txt, task_meta.json, and a reference_context.pdf. According to the task instructions, I should prioritize the text files and skip the PDF if there are any issues.",
  "plan": "First, I'll read the primary source files (conversation_notes.txt and task_meta.json) to understand the content I need to work with. Then I'll create the TED-style talk outline and speaker notes based on this content.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat conversation_notes.txt\\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat task_meta.json\\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I've read the primary source files. The conversation_notes.txt provides the core insight: teams explain what systems do but not why they matter, and the solution is turning abstract technical lessons into narratives with concrete examples. The task_meta.json specifies the required sections: Opening, Setup, Problem, Core Concept, Real-World Examples, Broader Implications, Closing, and Q&A Preparation. I should also check the skill_context directory for any additional guidance.",
  "plan": "Check the skill_context directory for additional context, then create the comprehensive TED-style talk outline with speaker notes based on all the information gathered. The talk needs to be 2200+ words for a 40-50 minute presentation.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la skill_context/\\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat skill_context/*\\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The skill_context contains a SKILL.md file with detailed guidance on creating TED-style talks. The output was truncated. I need to read the full content to understand the complete structure and requirements for the talk.",
  "plan": "Read the full SKILL.md file to get complete guidance on TED talk structure, then create the comprehensive output.txt file with all required sections.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat skill_context/SKILL.md\\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The output.txt file is now 22,577 bytes. I need to verify that the talk is complete and meets the requirements (2200+ words for 40-50 minute presentation, includes opening hook, narrative arc, real-world examples, broader implications, and Q&A section).",
  "plan": "Verify the file content by checking the word count and reviewing the structure to ensure all required sections are present.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "wc -w output.txt\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -100 output.txt\\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "tail -50 output.txt\\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The output.txt file has 3192 words, which exceeds the 2200+ word requirement for a 40-50 minute presentation. The file structure shows the opening section with a hook, and the head/tail commands confirm the file is properly formatted. Let me verify the complete structure by checking for all required sections.",
  "plan": "Verify that all required sections are present in the file by searching for section headers.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -E '(OPENING|SETUP|PROBLEM|CORE CONCEPT|REAL-WORLD|IMPLICATIONS|CLOSING|Q&A)' output.txt\\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}
